    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let us examine the conversation line by line.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Никто не видел запасного ключа?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Has anyone seen the spare key?' The verb for 'saw' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "А мы не знаем.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We do not know.' Senya links 'and' directly with 'we' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "leva",
      "t": "И не видели.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And we have not seen it.' Here 'saw' uses the plural past-tense ending, which also serves for the polite 'you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Но вообще...",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But, generally speaking...' the inspector links 'but' directly with 'at all' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Ничем не смогу вам помочь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am afraid I cannot help you.' The negative particle sits directly before the verb for 'will be able to' to negate the action.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Мои сотрудники потеряли ключ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'My staff lost the key.' Notice how 'my' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'key'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Экая досада.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a shame.' Chef Viktor combines 'what a' with 'annoyance' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ой, ой, ой.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Oh, oh, oh.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'oh' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "А как же нам быть?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'So what are we to do?' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'and' with 'be'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У меня есть запасной ключ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I have a spare key.' To express possession, Russian uses the preposition 'by' plus the genitive pronoun followed by 'key'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я всегда дубликаты храню.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I always keep a duplicate.' Chef Viktor combines 'I' with 'to keep' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А, вот он, ключ.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Ah, there is the key.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'he' straight to 'key'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "nastya",
      "t": "Нашелся ключ?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Found the key?' The verb for 'to turn up' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
